I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle._grounding_kernel. Its documented rule says: "The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on." The rule requires no relaxation, so a span must occur in the document exactly as written, apart from no case or date-range relaxation. The two reply texts are: first, {"value": "a", "span": "a  b", "document": "a b"}; second, {"value": "a", "span": "a b", "document": "a b"}. For the first, the rule requires SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT, because the span 'a  b' does not occur in the document 'a b' without relaxing whitespace. For the second, the rule requires GROUNDED, because the span 'a b' occurs in the document 'a b' and the value 'a' is in the span. The function _grounding_kernel normalises whitespace in _span_occurs even when relaxations is empty, so it returns GROUNDED for both texts. That is the same answer on a pair the rule requires to be told apart. I would be wrong if _grounding_kernel returned SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT for the first text, or if the documented rule is read as allowing whitespace normalisation as part of verbatim matching.